Lets the core handle the Mini Player, a video's Picture in Picture window, as Arc's does
(//browser_core/hooks.h): a scroll moves it, ⌘ or Ctrl with a scroll, or a pinch, sizes it, and a
double-click goes back to its tab. Chrome makes the window's background a caption, so the OS drags
it and the window never hears the press; while the core's hook is set it's the window's own, and
the core moves it as a caption would, through Widget::RunMoveLoop. The core also places it, the
first time 10 pt from the top right of the screen's work area, then where the last was left, which
it hears as each one closes or hides. It keeps that across launches in local state, in a pref of
its own that it registers through Chrome's RegisterLocalState. Once Chrome has laid out the
window's controls, the core gives it Dia's look: its corners, rim, scrim and top buttons, which it
reaches through the window's getters. Its controls fade over Dia's 200 ms, not Chrome's 250.

